#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.arg_docs
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.pf_docs
msgid "The §0 tactic introduces hypotheses (from the goal) into the proof state. Typical usage might look like the following. If the goal is:\n"
"§1\n"
"Then §2 will introduce the hypothesis §3 into the proof state.\n"
"\n"
"### **Universal generalization**\n"
"The §4 tactic is also good for introducing generic variables associated to a universally quantified goal. For example, if our goal is of the form:\n"
"§5\n"
"Then\n"
"§6\n"
"will make §7 an object and our goal becomes §8."
msgstr ""

#: Game
msgid "Intro to Proofs Game"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.SetWorld_eg.set_eg_docs
msgid "doc"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `obtain`
#. §1: `obtain`
#. §2: `obtain`
#. §3: `exact`
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.L02_arg
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"The §0 tactic is *very* handy especially when you'll need to use the assumption introduced by §1 in more intricate ways. But if you find yourself using §2 to introduce a hypothesis to only then call the tactic §3 on that hypothesis, then you probably could have made your work a little shorter."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.QuantifierWorld_eg.L05_quant
msgid "Forall distributes over ands"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L04_equiv
msgid "Great, we're almost home. Try to to finish this level off on your own."
msgstr ""

#. §0: ```
#. Q ∧ (_ ∧ _)
#. ```
#. §1: `R`
#. §2: `S`
#. §3: `← And_assoc`
#. §4: `(Q ∧ R) ∧ S`
#. §5: `Q ∧ (R ∧ S)`
#. §6: `(Q ∧ R) ∧ S`
#. §7: `S ∧ (Q ∧ R)`
#. §8: ```
#. rw[And_comm (Q ∧ R)]
#. ```
#. §9: ```
#. rw[And_comm (Q ∧ R) S]
#. ```
#. §10: ```
#. rw[And_comm _ S]
#. ```
#. §11: `∧`
#. §12: `\\wedge`
#. §13: `\\and`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L04_equiv
msgid "**Here's what happened:** Lean searched for the **first instance** of\n"
"§0\n"
"where the underscores are wildcards. The first underscore was matched with §1, the second with §2. Lean then applied §3 (from ***right-to-left***) to this instance. In particular, Lean substituted §4 for §5.\n"
"\n"
"Now we'd like to **commute** the expression §6 to get §7. 👉 We can accomplish this with either:\n"
"§8\n"
"or\n"
"§9\n"
"or even\n"
"§10\n"
"This last form is slightly dangerous in this case though; 🤔 can you tell why?\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting Tip**\n"
"In order to pretty-print symbols like §11 use §12 or §13."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.L02_arg
msgid "Modus Tolens"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P → R`
#. §1: `P`
#. §2: `P → Q`
#. §3: `intro`
#. §4: `intro`
#. §5: `intro`
#. §6: ```
#. intro hP
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.L06_arg
msgid "We wish to prove that §0 -- our goal, itself, is an implication. We know that implications are true by default if the hypothesis is false. So we might as well assume that §1 is true (otherwise §2 is definitely true). How do we do this in Lean?\n"
"### **❯ The §3 tactic**\n"
"To *introduce* the hypothesis of an implication as a goal, we use the §4 tactic. 🔍 Check out the entry for §5 in the **Tactics** tab. 👉 In our specific situation, try:\n"
"§6\n"
""
msgstr ""

